SAS provides several options to output PDF files in a variety of formats, including journal format. Here are the steps to follow:

**Method 1: Using the `ods pdf` statement**

You can use the `ods pdf` statement to output a PDF file in a journal format. The syntax is as follows:
```
ods pdf file="path\to\file.pdf";
ods pdf startpage=never;
title "Journal Title";
footnote "Journal Footnote";
ods pdf endglobe;
proc print ...;
run;
ods pdf close;
```
In this example:

* `ods pdf file="path\to\file.pdf"` specifies the output file name and location.
* `ods pdf startpage=never` tells SAS not to create a title page.
* `title "Journal Title"` sets the title of the journal.
* `footnote "Journal Footnote"` sets the footnote of the journal.
* `ods pdf endglobe` marks the end of the mathematical expression.
* `proc print ...;` runs your SAS code to generate the print output.
* `ods pdf close` closes the PDF file.

**Method 2: Using the `ods sulfur` statement**

You can also use the `ods sulfur` statement to output a PDF file in a journal format. The syntax is as follows:
```
ods sulfur file="path\to\file.pdf";
ods sulfur journal;
title "Journal Title";
footnote "Journal Footnote";
ods sulfur end;
proc print ...;
run;
ods sulfur close;
```
In this example:

* `ods sulfur file="path\to\file.pdf"` specifies the output file name and location.
* `ods sulfur journal` specifies the journal format.
* `title "Journal Title"` sets the title of the journal.
* `footnote "Journal Footnote"` sets the footnote of the journal.
* `ods sulfur end` marks the end of the journal.
* `proc print ...;` runs your SAS code to generate the print output.
* `ods sulfur close` closes the PDF file.

